WEBVTT

cue-VID_U2_04-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Communication is the transferring of a message from the sender to the receiver, who understands the message. At Harbourview Solutions Call Centre, a shift handover note is left on a desk. The evening team misses eighteen callbacks because the note is incomplete. The sender thought it was clear. The receiver never saw the crucial change. Watch the note stop and restart.

cue-VID_U2_04-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Five steps animate. The sender encodes an idea into a message. The message travels through a channel: email, phone, briefing or noticeboard. The receiver decodes it. Feedback is the reply from the receiver confirming the message was understood and acted upon. A red noise barrier blocks the channel. A read-back arrow loops around the wall and returns confirmation. Without feedback, the sender never knows the message failed.

cue-VID_U2_04-3
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Methods split three ways. Verbal communication uses speech; non-verbal uses gestures, facial expression and body language. Formal communication follows official channels; informal grows through conversation. Internal communication is between members of the same organisation. External communication reaches customers, suppliers, investors and the public. Harbourview uses a briefing for staff and a letter for a supplier. Audience and purpose should shape the medium.

cue-VID_U2_04-4
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Compare the channels. Meetings give instant feedback but cost travel time. Email costs two cents and leaves a record, but invites overload. A text costs five cents and is quick, but too short for detail. A letter costs eighty-five cents and suits formal external notice, but is slow. Social media reaches many cheaply, but messages spread widely. Each method trades speed, cost, reach and record.

cue-VID_U2_04-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Barriers stop effective communication. Physical noise comes from two hundred calls an hour. Language barriers arise from unfamiliar words. Jargon causes the same problem: a note saying SLA breach means little without explanation. Cultural differences affect how directly a person refuses a request. Each barrier breaks the loop at a different point, so each needs a different fix.

cue-VID_U2_04-6
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Information overload is another barrier. A twenty-seven page manual hides one safety step. The wrong medium worsens it: a diagram sent by text cannot be read. Mitigation is concrete. Use plain language and define jargon. Break long guidance into short numbered steps. Choose a channel that fits the message, then ask the receiver to read it back. Feedback and checking turn a one-way send into shared understanding.

cue-VID_U2_04-7
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Harbourview changes its handover. Because handwritten notes were ambiguous, therefore the team uses a structured digital checklist with a five minute briefing, so missed callbacks fall from eighteen to four. Complaint rates drop from six percent to two percent. First contact resolution rises from fifty-eight percent to seventy-four percent. It has eight fields and a read-back box. Agents tick each item before leaving. Every dashboard metric moves as the loop closes.

cue-VID_U2_04-8
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Evaluate the channels. Email is cheap, searchable and leaves a record, but long threads create overload and tone is easily misread. Briefings allow questions and cues, but cost time across shifts. Texts are fast and cheap, but too brief for complex instructions. Letters and formal reports are slow, yet they suit external contracts. The best channel depends on urgency, sensitivity and whether a written record is needed. Often the answer combines two channels.

cue-VID_U2_04-9
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Recap the loop: sender, message, channel, receiver, feedback. Noise and barriers can break it at any stage. Internal messages stay inside the firm; external messages reach outside. Verbal, non-verbal, formal and informal methods all have a place. Harbourview cut missed callbacks by fourteen a day using a checklist and read-back. For an answer, name the barrier, show its effect, then give one concrete mitigation and one channel trade-off. That is application, not just a list of apps.

